# React Components: Understanding the Mechanics (Part II)

The freact we read in Part I turns one element into one DOM node. Two React features break that assumption, and both break it the same way: sometimes a description has to become **several** nodes, or none.

In the video, we added both to freact: **fragments**, and **arrays of elements**. The updated code is in `freact/02-fragments/` next to this notebook. It is the same project as Part I's `freact/01-elements/` with a handful of additions, and this reading walks through exactly the parts that changed. To watch it run, right-click `index.html` in that folder and choose "Show Preview".

## The Problem: One Node Is Not Always Enough

A component returns a single value, so returning two elements has to be wrapped in something:

```jsx
return (
  <div>
    <h1>Title</h1>
    <p>Body</p>
  </div>
);
```

That `<div>` is not wanted. It exists only to satisfy the one-value rule, and it lands in the DOM, where it can interfere with layout and with CSS selectors that expect a particular structure.

React's answer is a **fragment**: empty angle brackets that group children without producing an element.

```jsx
return (
  <>
    <h1>Title</h1>
    <p>Body</p>
  </>
);
```

In the video, writing `<>` before freact supported it produced an element whose tag was literally `undefined`. So the first job is giving fragments an identity of their own.

## A Type That Cannot Collide

A fragment needs a `type` that can never be confused with a tag name or a component, and JavaScript has exactly the tool: **`Symbol`**, which creates a value guaranteed to be unique and unequal to everything else.

```{javascript autorun}
const Fragment = Symbol('Fragment');
const other = Symbol('Fragment');

console.log('Symbols with the same label are still different: ' + (Fragment === other));
console.log('And nothing else equals one: ' + (Fragment === 'Fragment'));
```

That uniqueness is the whole point: a `<div>` has type `'div'`, and no element can accidentally have type `Fragment`.

## What Changed in `freact.js`

```{walkthrough}
title: The additions to freact.js
Everything from Part I is still here, unchanged. These are the new pieces, in the order the video added them.

step: The Fragment marker
file: freact/02-fragments/freact.js
region: fragment
One line, at the top of the file. The compiler turns `<>...</>` into `createElement(Fragment, ...)`, so from `createElement`'s point of view a fragment is an ordinary element whose type happens to be this symbol.
@"Symbol('Fragment')": In the video this line was written `export const Fragment = ...`; with no build step it is a plain constant, shared through the globals at the bottom of the file.

step: A fragment produces its children, not itself
file: freact/02-fragments/freact.js
region: fragment-branch
A new branch near the top of `_createDomNode`. When the element is a fragment, no node is created for it: its children are each built with the same function, and the resulting **array** of nodes is returned directly.
@"element.type === Fragment": Comparing against the symbol. This test can never be fooled by a component or a tag name, which is what the Symbol bought us.
@"props.children.map": `map(...)`, from "Rendering Lists With Map", turns the array of child descriptions into an array of child nodes, and `.flat()` unpacks any child that itself produced several nodes.

step: An array of elements gets the same treatment
file: freact/02-fragments/freact.js
region: array-of-elements
The other new branch, and it is the same shape. A JSX expression like `{ names.map(...) }` hands the renderer an array of descriptions where one element was expected, so `_createDomNode` maps itself over it.
@"Array.isArray(element)": An array is not an element, so this check comes first, before anything tries to read `element.type` off of it.

step: Appending several nodes
file: freact/02-fragments/freact.js
region: children
The children loop from Part I appended one node per child. Now that a child can be a fragment or an array, what comes back may be an array of nodes, so appending gets a check.
@"Array.isArray(childDom)": Both shapes are handled: an array of nodes is appended one by one, a single node as before.

step: render learns the same trick
file: freact/02-fragments/freact.js
region: render
The top-level element can be a fragment or an array too, so `render` gets the matching check, and several nodes land side by side in the container.
@"Array.isArray(domNode)": This is why the fragment's children end up directly inside `#root`, with no wrapper element anywhere.
```

## The Components

With the machinery in place, the component side can finally be written the way you have been writing real React.

```{walkthrough}
title: App.jsx, using both features
step: A reusable Greeting
file: freact/02-fragments/App.jsx
region: greeting-component
A component made to be rendered by another component. In the video this lived in its own file, `Greeting.jsx`, exported there and imported by App; with no build step, both components share this file, and the comment marks where the import would go.
@"props.name": The same props mechanism from Part I: `_createDomNode` sees a function type, calls it, and passes `{ name: 'Alice' }` in. The `|| 'visitor'` supplies a default when the prop is missing.

step: App renders a fragment and a list
file: freact/02-fragments/App.jsx
region: app-component
Both new features in five lines: everything is grouped by `<>...</>` with no wrapper element, and the list of Greetings is data-driven, one per name.
@"return <>": The fragment. Change it to a `<div>...</div>`, save, reload the preview, and inspect the page: the extra wrapper appears in the DOM. Change it back and it is gone.
@"names.map": An array of element descriptions, straight into the JSX. This is the pattern from "Rendering Lists With Map", and the array branch you just read is the reason it works.
@"<Greeting name={n}": One note: there are no `key` props in freact. Keys exist so *real* React can tell list items apart between renders; freact rebuilds everything from scratch anyway, so it has nothing to tell apart.
```

Open the preview and count what is inside `#root`: an `h1`, a `p`, and three `div`s, side by side. Five nodes from one component, and no wrapper in sight. Then add a fourth name to the `names` array, save, and reload: four Greetings, and the number is not written anywhere in the markup.

## What This Explains

Two things you have already been told now have reasons.

**Why a fragment produces no element**: because `_createDomNode` returns its children's nodes instead of making one of its own.

**Why you can put an array of elements straight into JSX**: because an array is a case the renderer handles, not something you have to unpack first.

Both come from loosening one assumption, that a description makes exactly one node. Real React's version of this is more elaborate and the shape is the same.

## Exercises

1. Complete the fragment branch. A fragment should return the nodes of its children, flattened, and no node of its own. (This cell reaches the real document as `window.document`, since a cell's own `document` has no `createElement`.)

    ```{javascript}
    const Fragment = Symbol('Fragment');
    const realDocument = window.document;

    function _createDomNode(element) {
      if (!element) {
        return null;
      }

      if (element.type === Fragment) {
        // TODO: map the fragment's children through _createDomNode and
        // flatten the result, so several nodes come back instead of one
      }

      const dom = realDocument.createElement(element.type);

      for (const child of element.props.children) {
        const childDom = _createDomNode(child);

        if (Array.isArray(childDom)) {
          for (const node of childDom) {
            dom.append(node);
          }
        } else if (childDom) {
          dom.append(childDom);
        }
      }

      return dom;
    }

    const tree = {
      type: 'section',
      props: {
        children: [
          {
            type: Fragment,
            props: {
              children: [
                { type: 'h1', props: { children: [] } },
                { type: 'p', props: { children: [] } }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    };

    const result = _createDomNode(tree);

    console.log(result.outerHTML);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const Fragment = Symbol('Fragment');
    const realDocument = window.document;

    function _createDomNode(element) {
      if (!element) {
        return null;
      }

      if (element.type === Fragment) {
        return element.props.children.map(_createDomNode).flat();
      }

      const dom = realDocument.createElement(element.type);

      for (const child of element.props.children) {
        const childDom = _createDomNode(child);

        if (Array.isArray(childDom)) {
          for (const node of childDom) {
            dom.append(node);
          }
        } else if (childDom) {
          dom.append(childDom);
        }
      }

      return dom;
    }

    const tree = {
      type: 'section',
      props: {
        children: [
          {
            type: Fragment,
            props: {
              children: [
                { type: 'h1', props: { children: [] } },
                { type: 'p', props: { children: [] } }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    };

    const result = _createDomNode(tree);

    console.log(result.outerHTML);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(result && result.tagName).should('equal', 'SECTION').run('The outer element should still become a `section` node', 'Built the outer element');
    wrap(result && result.children.length).should('equal', 2).run('Both of the fragment`s children should end up inside the section', 'Both children were appended');
    wrap(result && result.querySelectorAll('h1').length).should('equal', 1).run('The `h1` should be there', 'The h1 is there');
    wrap(result && result.outerHTML.indexOf('undefined')).should('equal', -1).run('A fragment should produce no element of its own, so no `<undefined>` tag should appear', 'The fragment added no element');
    ```

2. Build a list from data. Turn each name into an element description, and let the array branch do the rest.

    ```{javascript}
    const realDocument = window.document;

    function _createDomNode(element) {
      if (Array.isArray(element)) {
        return element.map(_createDomNode).flat();
      }

      const dom = realDocument.createElement(element.type);

      dom.innerText = element.props.text || '';

      return dom;
    }

    const names = ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie'];

    // TODO: turn names into an array of element descriptions of the form
    // { type: 'li', props: { text: <the name> } }
    const items = [];

    const nodes = _createDomNode(items);

    console.log(nodes.length + ' nodes');
    ```
    ```+solution
    const realDocument = window.document;

    function _createDomNode(element) {
      if (Array.isArray(element)) {
        return element.map(_createDomNode).flat();
      }

      const dom = realDocument.createElement(element.type);

      dom.innerText = element.props.text || '';

      return dom;
    }

    const names = ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie'];

    const items = names.map(function (name) {
      return { type: 'li', props: { text: name } };
    });

    const nodes = _createDomNode(items);

    console.log(nodes.length + ' nodes');
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(Array.isArray(nodes)).should('equal', true).run('An array of descriptions should produce an array of nodes', 'Got an array of nodes');
    wrap(nodes.length).should('equal', 3).run('There should be one node per name, so three', 'One node per name');
    wrap(nodes[0] && nodes[0].tagName).should('equal', 'LI').run('Each description should have a `type` of `li`', 'Built list items');
    wrap(nodes[2] && nodes[2].innerText).should('equal', 'Charlie').run('Each description should carry its own name in `props.text`', 'Carried the names through');
    ```

3. Why a `Symbol` and not a string

    ```{mcq}
    freact marks fragments with `Symbol('Fragment')`. What would go wrong with a plain string like `'fragment'` instead?
    correct: Correct! A `Symbol` is guaranteed unique, so no element type can ever equal it by accident. A string could collide with a real tag name or with a component someone else wrote.
    wrong: Element types are compared with `===`. What else in a real application might equal the string `'fragment'`?
    [x] A string could collide with a real element type; a `Symbol` cannot be equal to anything else
    [ ] Strings cannot be used in a `switch` or an `if`
    [ ] React reserves all lowercase strings for HTML tags
    [ ] Symbols render faster than strings
    ```

4. What comes back for a fragment

    ```{mcq}
    A fragment element goes into `_createDomNode`. What comes out?
    correct: Correct! An array of its children's nodes and nothing wrapping them, which is why the children land directly in the parent and no extra element appears in the DOM.
    wrong: A fragment exists precisely so that no element of its own shows up. Look back at "A fragment produces its children, not itself" in the walkthrough.
    [x] An array of the nodes its children produced, with no node of its own
    [ ] A single empty `<div>` containing its children
    [ ] `null`, so its children are skipped
    [ ] A text node holding the children's text
    ```

